Claude Code and Codex both start every session cold. Claude Code's own auto memory helps, but its docs say it is machine-local and loads only the first 200 lines or 25KB of MEMORY.md, so anything beyond that, or anything on another laptop, is gone. A memory MCP server (or a memory plugin that ships one) fills that gap by storing what you decided, fixed and learned, then handing the relevant parts back to the agent next time.

There are now dozens of options, and they solve different problems. This post compares ten that you can wire into Claude Code, Codex and Cursor today. Short version: claude-mem is the most popular and the easiest automatic option, Basic Memory is the best if you want notes you can read and edit, Mem0 and Supermemory are the hosted APIs with the most polish, and the official MCP memory server is the tiny, free baseline. Star counts, prices and release dates below are as of November 26, 2026. This is MCP servers and coding-agent plugins only; for memory frameworks and APIs you build apps on, see AgentPedia's [best AI agent memory tools of 2026](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/best-ai-agent-memory-tools-2026).

{% youtube id="Mzmzy-vutSo" title="Mem0 + Claude Code MCP: Give Your AI Agent a Permanent Memory" /%}

Third-party walkthrough of adding Mem0's hosted memory MCP server to Claude Code, from the Prompt Engineer 48 channel. I confirmed the title and channel through YouTube's oEmbed endpoint, but I did not watch it end to end and did not verify its upload date, so use it as a visual companion to the setup steps below and not as a source for any claim.

## Short answer: quick picks

| If you want | Pick | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Best overall for Claude Code and Codex | claude-mem | Automatic capture through hooks, local by default, Apache-2.0, 119,949 stars |
| Best free and open source, readable memory | Basic Memory | Plain Markdown notes you can edit, MCP server, AGPL-3.0, free locally |
| Best hosted memory API | Mem0 | Hosted MCP endpoint, plugins for Claude Code and Codex, free plan with your own LLM key |
| Best for one memory across many tools | Supermemory | MCP URL, plugins for Claude Code and Cursor, shared per-repo container, MIT core |
| Best for a temporal knowledge graph | Graphiti / Zep | Facts with validity windows, self-hosted MCP server, Apache-2.0 |
| Best knowledge graph with coding-agent plugins | Cognee | Claude Code and Codex plugins, 100M free tokens on the Standard plan |
| Best lightweight, single binary | Engram | Go binary, SQLite and FTS5, MCP server, MIT |
| Best hook-heavy local system | agentmemory | 12 hooks, 54 MCP tools, keyless BM25 search |
| Best baseline you can read in an afternoon | MCP memory server (official) | Knowledge-graph JSONL file, nine tools, reference implementation |
| Best if you want a stateful-agent platform | Letta | Self-editing memory, but it replaces your harness rather than plugging into it |

{% callout type="info" %}
Ranking is my judgment from public sources, not a benchmark. No neutral, reproducible benchmark compares these ten for coding-agent use, so I weighted install friction, Claude Code and Codex support, openness, and how much each vendor documents about limits. Where a number is vendor-reported I say so.
{% /callout %}

