## 1. claude-mem: the most popular automatic option

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/claude-mem.png" name="claude-mem" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** claude-mem is an Apache-2.0 plugin by Alex Newman (thedotmack). Its README describes it as a second agent that runs beside your coding agent and takes notes while it works. The next session starts with a short index of what matters and pulls details in only when needed. The repository had 119,949 stars on November 26, 2026 and shipped release v17.0.1 that same day, so it is both the biggest and one of the most actively maintained projects here.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want memory without managing it: you install once, work normally, and your second session in a project opens with memory from the first. It supports Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode and more; the README says Copilot CLI, Goose, Roo Code, Warp and Zed can search your memory but their sessions are not recorded.

**Key features.**
- Hook-based capture of what the agent does, compressed by an observer model into observations.
- A mem-search skill that searches history in three steps (index, timeline, details), so the agent spends tokens only on what it opens. The catalog has it as [mem-search](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mem-search).
- An "MCP Search Tools" section in the README, plus SQLite FTS5 and Chroma hybrid search described in the docs.
- Memory lives in `~/.claude-mem` on your machine; cloud sync is opt-in.

**Pros**
- Zero-effort capture: you do not have to ask the model to save anything.
- Local by default, Apache-2.0, enormous community and a fast release cadence.
- Works across Claude Code, Codex and Cursor from one installer.

**Cons**
- Anonymous usage analytics are on by default; `npx claude-mem telemetry disable` turns them off.
- It captures a lot, and you curate what matters afterwards.
- It runs a local worker service, which is more surface area than a single stdio MCP server. The v16.0.0 release notes (November 18, 2026) describe removing a read-only remote TV page and binding the worker to localhost when a non-loopback host is configured without a token. That is a fix, but it shows why you should read release notes for anything that opens a port.
- `npm install -g claude-mem` installs only the SDK, not the plugin; the README warns to use `npx claude-mem install` or the marketplace.

**Benefits for your use case.** For a Claude Code or Codex user the benefit is continuity across sessions with no change in habit, plus searchable history when you ask "did we already solve this?".

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, claude-mem.ai/pricing).** The open-source engine is free. Cloud Sync, which carries memory between devices, is $12 per month with a 30-day trial (no card needed), or $99 per year with no trial. Optional hosted generation plans are Pro at $30 per month ($30 allowance), Heavy at $70 per month ($49 allowance) and CMEM Max at $100 per month ($70 allowance). Hosted tokens and Cloud Sync are separate subscriptions. TeamBrain, the shared-memory product, has no public price.

**Free tier / trial.** Free local engine; Cloud Sync 30-day trial. Model or provider usage for the observer may carry its own cost depending on configuration, per the pricing page.

**Standout use case.** Long-running projects across several machines, where you want to ask the agent what you tried last week.

**Skip it if** you want a small, auditable stdio server with a handful of tools, or if a persistent background worker and default telemetry are not acceptable in your environment.

**Setup (from the README).**

```bash
npx claude-mem install

/plugin marketplace add thedotmack/claude-mem
/plugin install claude-mem
```

For Codex and Cursor, run `npx claude-mem install` and pick them in the prompt. To target one agent directly use `--ide <name>`; the README says the full list of values is in `npx claude-mem --help`, and I did not verify the exact Codex and Cursor values, so check that output.

{% figure src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thedotmack/claude-mem/main/docs/public/cm-preview.gif" alt="claude-mem preview animation from the project repository" caption="Preview animation from the claude-mem repository. Source: github.com/thedotmack/claude-mem" /%}

## 2. Basic Memory: notes you can actually read

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/basic-memory.png" name="Basic Memory" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Basic Memory is an MCP server and knowledge base built on plain Markdown files. Your AI and you both read and write the same notes; a local SQLite index provides search. It is licensed AGPL-3.0, had 4,719 GitHub stars on November 26, 2026, and its latest tagged release is v1.0.0 (October 29, 2026). The README refers to tools with `bm_` names, for example `bm_edit` and `bm_write`, and to schema tools such as `bm_schema_infer`.

**Who it is for.** People who want to see and edit what the AI remembers, who already keep notes in Obsidian-style Markdown, or who want one knowledge base shared across Claude, Codex, Cursor and ChatGPT.

**Key features.**
- Local install through `uv tool install basic-memory --prerelease=allow` (the flag is required, per the README).
- Hybrid full-text and vector search with FastEmbed embeddings, per the README, plus Markdown observations and `[[wikilink]]` relations that the README calls a knowledge graph.
- A Claude Code plugin installed with `bm install claude-code`, adding a session-start briefing, a checkpoint before context compaction, and `/basic-memory:*` commands.
- Cloud version with web, mobile and desktop access and bidirectional sync to local files.

**Pros**
- Files stay the source of truth, so you can diff, back up and edit memory with normal tools.
- Free to run locally, with all data on your disk.
- Explicit setup instructions for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and VS Code in the README.

**Cons**
- The model has to decide to call the tools, so notes are only as good as what gets written; capture is mostly agent-written with an opt-in capture mode.
- AGPL-3.0 matters if you plan to embed or modify it in a hosted product.
- Requires Python through `uv`, and the `--prerelease=allow` requirement is an easy thing to miss.

**Benefits for your use case.** Decisions, architecture notes and gotchas live in a folder you control, and they carry into Codex or Cursor sessions because they are plain files behind a standard MCP server.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, basicmemory.com/pricing).** Cloud $19 per month per workspace (1,900 credits included), Pro $99 per month (9,900 credits, up to 5 members) and Business $249 per month (24,900 credits, up to 25 members). One credit is $0.01. A Black Friday offer of 50 percent off each month for 12 months with code BLACKFRIDAY ends Monday, November 30 at 11:59 PM PT. Note the repository README still says "$15.00/mo locked for life", which contradicts the $19 on the pricing page; I used the pricing page.

**Free tier / trial.** Open source and free locally; cloud starts with a 7-day trial.

**Standout use case.** A research or architecture journal that both you and the agent maintain, readable in any editor.

**Skip it if** you want fully automatic capture of everything the agent does, or you cannot accept AGPL terms.

**Setup (from the README).**

```bash
uv tool install basic-memory --prerelease=allow

claude mcp add basic-memory -- uvx --prerelease=allow basic-memory mcp
bm install claude-code   # optional plugin, needs the MCP server first
```

```toml
[mcp_servers.basic-memory]
command = "uvx"
args = ["--prerelease=allow", "basic-memory", "mcp"]
```

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "basic-memory": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["--prerelease=allow", "basic-memory", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

The JSON block goes in `.cursor/mcp.json` (project) or `~/.cursor/mcp.json` (global).

## 3. Mem0: hosted memory with a Codex and Claude Code plugin

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/mem0.png" name="Mem0" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Mem0 is a memory layer for AI agents. The open-source core (mem0ai/mem0, Apache-2.0) had 69,508 stars on November 26, 2026. The hosted Mem0 Platform exposes an MCP server at `https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp`. The docs list tools including `add_memory`, `search_memories`, `get_memories`, `delete_memory`, `delete_all_memories` and `delete_entities`.

**A naming caveat.** The topic is often searched as "OpenMemory MCP". That local, self-run OpenMemory is sunset: a merged pull request (#6530, July 29, 2026) removed the `openmemory/` directory from the Mem0 monorepo and points self-hosters to the `server/` directory instead (FastAPI, PostgreSQL with pgvector, Neo4j). The `mem0ai/openmemory` repository now describes itself as an open-source CLI and TUI for porting coding sessions across Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode, with 108 stars. So today's Mem0 route for coding agents is the hosted MCP endpoint or the plugins.

**Who it is for.** Teams that want a managed memory backend with a clean MCP endpoint and are happy to hold an API key.

**Key features.**
- Claude Code plugin (version 0.3.1 per the docs) with automatic capture and recall, installed from the Mem0 marketplace.
- Codex plugin with automatic capture, automatic recall, a search tool and six memory skills, connecting to Mem0's cloud over MCP.
- `npx mcp-add` helper that writes the server into several clients' config files.
- The site mentions HIPAA and GDPR readiness; these are vendor statements and I did not review the compliance documents.

**Pros**
- Easiest hosted path: one URL, one key, documented client-by-client.
- Large open-source community behind the core library.
- Free plan with generous monthly caps if you bring your own LLM key.

**Cons**
- You need a Mem0 account and API key; data goes to their cloud.
- Graph memory, user profiles and the Dream consolidation feature are listed only on the Custom plan.
- The pricing page I fetched on November 26, 2026 shows only Free and Custom, so there is no published mid-tier price to budget against.

**Benefits for your use case.** One memory store reachable from Claude Code, Codex and Cursor with the same key, without running a database yourself.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, mem0.ai/pricing).** Free: unlimited end users; for the first 30 days, 10,000 adds and 1,000 retrievals a month with model calls on Mem0; with your own LLM key, 50,000 adds and 10,000 retrievals a month; usage-based billing past the limits with a monthly cap you set; community support. Custom: limits set to your volume, graph memory, user profiles, Dream, advanced analytics, private Slack support, SSO, audit logs, SLA and on-prem, price on request.

**Free tier / trial.** The Free plan above; no separate trial is advertised.

**Standout use case.** A shared team memory API that also powers your own product.

**Skip it if** you need everything on your machine, or you want the old local OpenMemory server, which is sunset.

**Setup (from docs.mem0.ai).**

```bash
npx mcp-add \
  --name mem0-mcp \
  --type http \
  --url "https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp" \
  --clients "claude code"

export MEM0_API_KEY='your-mem0-api-key'
claude plugin marketplace add mem0ai/mem0
claude plugin install mem0@mem0-plugins --scope user --config api_key="$MEM0_API_KEY"
unset MEM0_API_KEY
```

```bash
codex mcp add mem0 --url https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp/ --bearer-token-env-var MEM0_API_KEY
```

```toml
[mcp_servers.mem0]
url = "https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp/"
bearer_token_env_var = "MEM0_API_KEY"
```

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "mem0-mcp": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

The JSON block is the Cursor config. The docs warn not to combine the Codex plugin with the direct MCP block, because the plugin registers `mem0` itself and you would get a duplicate.

