## 4. Supermemory: one memory across every agent you use

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/supermemory.png" name="Supermemory" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Supermemory calls itself the memory and context layer for AI. The GitHub repository (supermemoryai/supermemory, MIT) had 31,837 stars on November 26, 2026. It ships a hosted memory API, a hosted MCP server at `https://mcp.supermemory.ai/mcp`, open-source plugins for Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, OpenCode, OpenClaw, Hermes and Muse Code, and a local mode you can run yourself.

**Who it is for.** People who use several coding agents and want one memory they all read and write, plus teams that may later want a memory API inside their own product.

**Key features.**
- MCP server that works with Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf, VS Code, Claude Code, OpenCode, OpenClaw and Hermes, per the README. Its `context` tool injects your profile and recent activity at the start of a session; in Cursor and Claude Code you can type `/context`.
- Separate open-source plugin repositories: `claude-supermemory`, `cursor-supermemory` and `codex-supermemory`.
- A memory graph: Supermemory's October 21, 2026 post says every document becomes atomic facts and that old versions of updated facts stay as history.
- Local mode: `curl -fsSL https://supermemory.ai/install | bash`, then `npx supermemory local`. The README says it serves the full Memory API on `http://localhost:6767`, with local embeddings by default (`Xenova/bge-base-en-v1.5`) and optional Ollama for offline text memories.

**Pros**
- One URL gets any MCP client connected, and the free plan includes all the plugins.
- MIT core plus a local mode means you are not locked to the hosted service.
- Active: the repository was pushed to on November 26, 2026.

**Cons**
- Pricing is metered credits, not a flat fee, so cost depends on how much you store and search. Estimating a month takes some arithmetic.
- The README claims number one on LongMemEval, LoCoMo and ConvoMem. That is the vendor's own claim, and Mem0 and agentmemory publish their own benchmark claims too, so none of these can be compared directly.
- Local mode still downloads an embedding model on first run, and URL ingestion uses a hosted service according to the README.

**Benefits for your use case.** A single memory that follows you from Claude Code to Cursor to Codex without a separate store per tool, and a way to leave the hosted service later by changing a base URL.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, supermemory.ai/pricing).** Free $0 with $5 of usage included, all plugins, one team seat, unlimited end users. Pro $19 per month with $20 of usage, auto top-up and three seats. Max $100 per month with $130 of usage and a Gmail connector. Scale $399 per month with $600 of usage, advanced PDF extraction, S3 and web crawler connectors, unlimited seats, SOC 2, HIPAA BAA and a self-hosted option. Enterprise is custom. Listed rates per million: plain-text memory $5 in SM tokens, rich-content memory $10, SuperRAG text $1, SuperRAG rich media $2.

**Free tier / trial.** Free plan with $5 of usage; open-source local mode costs nothing but your hardware.

**Standout use case.** A personal or team memory shared across Claude Code, Cursor and Codex.

**Skip it if** you want a flat monthly price with no usage metering, or you want every byte on your own machine and do not want to depend on a vendor's hosted MCP endpoint.

**Setup (from the README, plus standard client syntax).**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "supermemory": {
      "url": "https://mcp.supermemory.ai/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

That JSON is the config the README gives for MCP clients, including Cursor (`.cursor/mcp.json`). For Claude Code, the equivalent one-liner uses Claude Code's standard HTTP transport flag with the same URL (my translation, not copied from Supermemory's docs):

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http supermemory https://mcp.supermemory.ai/mcp
```

Supermemory's own October 17, 2026 post advertises `npx supermemory plugins` as a one-command install for coding-agent plugins. For Codex, see the `codex-supermemory` repository.

