Supermemory's October 17, 2026 post announcing a one-command plugin install for coding agents.

{% x id="2112963898739765633" /%}

Supermemory's October 21, 2026 post on its memory graph, where updates keep the old version as history.

{% figure src="https://supermemory.ai/og/index.png" alt="Supermemory social preview image from supermemory.ai" caption="Supermemory's social preview image. Source: supermemory.ai" /%}

## 5. Graphiti / Zep: a temporal knowledge graph behind an MCP server

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/graphiti-zep.png" name="Graphiti / Zep" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Graphiti is Zep's open-source framework for building temporally aware knowledge graphs for AI agents (getzep/graphiti, Apache-2.0, 33,115 stars on November 26, 2026). Its `mcp_server` directory contains an MCP server, and the latest MCP release tag, `mcp-v1.1.5`, was published November 24, 2026. Zep is the managed product built on the same ideas.

**Who it is for.** Teams whose memory needs facts that change over time ("the API used v1 until March, then v2"), and who are comfortable running a graph database and supplying an LLM key.

**Key features.**
- Facts are edges with validity windows. The `search_memory_facts` tool supports `valid_at` and `invalid_at` date-range filters, and `add_memory` supports a bi-temporal `reference_time`.
- Tools listed in the README: `add_memory`, `add_triplet`, `search_nodes`, `search_memory_facts`, `summarize_saga`, `build_communities`, `get_episode_entities`, `delete_entity_edge` and more.
- FalkorDB is the default backend, bundled with the MCP server in one Docker container; Neo4j is the alternative via a separate compose file.
- HTTP transport at `http://localhost:8000/mcp/`, or stdio for clients that need it.

**Pros**
- Best fit here for evolving facts and provenance (`get_episode_entities` traces which episodes created which facts).
- Apache-2.0, self-hostable, and backed by an active company.
- Hybrid retrieval combining embeddings, BM25 and graph traversal, per the README.

**Cons**
- The README itself calls the `mcp_server` a reference implementation, so expect to read the code before depending on it.
- Heaviest setup in this list: Docker, a graph database and an LLM API key (the README's prerequisite lists an OpenAI key or another supported provider).
- It is not coding-agent specific. There is no Claude Code or Codex plugin in the docs I read; the documented clients are Claude Desktop, Cursor and VS Code with Copilot.
- Every write calls an LLM to extract entities and facts, so ingestion has a model cost.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your agent keeps contradicting itself because requirements changed, a graph that invalidates old facts rather than piling up conflicting notes is the right tool.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026).** Graphiti is free; you pay for your graph database and LLM calls. Zep (getzep.com/pricing) is credit based: Flex $125 per month with 50,000 credits (then $25 per 10,000), 600 requests per minute, 5 projects and 5 Context MCP Server seats; Flex Plus $375 per month with 200,000 credits (then $75 per 40,000), 1,000 requests per minute, 10 projects and 15 Context MCP Server seats. The page shows 17 percent savings on annual billing and custom Enterprise plans. Zep's Agent Skills are listed as free until January 4, 2027.

**Free tier / trial.** Zep: "Start free with 10,000 credits a month" for prototyping, with limits. Graphiti: free and open source.

**Standout use case.** A long-lived project where requirements, owners and decisions change, and you need the agent to know which fact is current.

**Skip it if** you want memory working in five minutes, or you do not want to run a database next to your editor.

**Setup (from the Graphiti MCP README).**

```bash
git clone https://github.com/getzep/graphiti.git
cd graphiti/mcp_server
docker compose up
```

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "graphiti-memory": {
      "transport": "http",
      "url": "http://localhost:8000/mcp/"
    }
  }
}
```

That block is the README's HTTP-client config and works in Cursor. For Claude Code and Codex I have not found official Graphiti snippets, so the equivalents below use each client's standard syntax with the same URL (my translation):

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http graphiti-memory http://localhost:8000/mcp/
```

```toml
[mcp_servers.graphiti-memory]
url = "http://localhost:8000/mcp/"
```

## 6. Cognee: knowledge-graph memory with Claude Code and Codex plugins

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/cognee.png" name="Cognee" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Cognee is an open-source AI memory engine (topoteretes/cognee, Apache-2.0, 33,649 stars on November 26, 2026, latest release v1.6.11 published that day). It turns documents and agent activity into a graph plus vectors you can query. Unlike most projects here it has first-party plugins for both Claude Code and Codex, and a separate MCP server in the `cognee-mcp` directory.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want graph-based memory and are fine running Python services, or paying for Cognee Cloud, to get it.

**Key features.**
- Claude Code plugin: `claude plugin marketplace add topoteretes/cognee-integrations`, then `claude plugin install cognee-memory@cognee`.
- Codex plugin: enable hooks with `codex features enable hooks`, then `codex plugin marketplace add topoteretes/cognee-integrations --ref main` and `codex plugin add cognee@cognee`.
- MCP server with a deliberately small surface: `remember`, `recall`, `forget` and a `cognify_status` progress check. It can store to a fast session cache or permanent graph memory, and adds a `code_search` tool for queries against an indexed code graph.
- Transports: stdio (default), Streamable HTTP and SSE; a Docker image `cognee/cognee-mcp:main`; or a cloud mode that connects to Cognee Cloud.

**Pros**
- The only graph-centric option here with maintained Claude Code and Codex plugins.
- Small MCP tool set, which the README says is intentional to save agent context.
- Free open-source engine and a free cloud plan with 100M tokens.

**Cons**
- More moving parts than a notes-based tool: a Python service, an LLM provider and storage backends.
- The README notes that `docker run` with `--transport http` will not work inside the container; you use `TRANSPORT_MODE` there. Easy to trip over.
- Plugin docs for local versus remote memory live in a separate integrations repository, so setup means reading two places.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your agent needs to answer "what depends on this module" or "what did we decide about X across these documents", a graph plus code search gets you further than keyword recall.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, cognee.ai/pricing).** Standard $0 per month with 100M tokens included (no card), then $0.10 per 1M tokens, one workspace included (more at $5 each per month), unlimited users and API calls, with Claude Code, Codex and MCP integrations. Premium $99 per month plus Standard token rates, adding auto-ontology and an ontology editor. Enterprise is a bring-your-own-cloud engagement with custom pricing, and its feature list includes bi-temporal memory and conflict resolution.

**Free tier / trial.** Standard at $0 with 100M tokens; the open-source engine is free to run yourself.

**Standout use case.** Codebase and document memory where relationships matter more than recency.

**Skip it if** you want a single binary or a folder of Markdown, or if you do not want an LLM extracting a graph from your data.

**Setup (from the Cognee README).**

```bash
claude plugin marketplace add topoteretes/cognee-integrations
claude plugin install cognee-memory@cognee

codex features enable hooks
codex plugin marketplace add topoteretes/cognee-integrations --ref main
codex plugin add cognee@cognee
```

Cursor and other MCP clients are pointed to the Cognee MCP guide and the `cognee-mcp` README rather than a copy-paste snippet. From source, the MCP server README runs `python src/server.py --transport http --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --path /mcp`.

{% youtube id="8hmqS2Y5RVQ" title="Cognee - Knowledge Engine for AI Agent Memory" /%}

Cognee's own channel introducing the project (title and channel confirmed by oEmbed; I did not watch it in full).

{% figure src="https://www.cognee.ai/images/meta/cognee-social-card.png" alt="Cognee social card from cognee.ai" caption="Cognee's social card. Source: cognee.ai" /%}

## 7. Engram: a single Go binary and a memory protocol

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/engram.png" name="Engram" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Engram (Gentleman-Programming/engram, MIT) describes itself as persistent memory for AI coding agents: an agent-agnostic Go binary with SQLite and FTS5, an MCP server, an HTTP API, a CLI and a TUI. It had 8,328 stars on November 26, 2026, and the latest release, v3.7.2, was published November 14, 2026.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want a small local tool with explicit save-and-recall behaviour and no cloud dependency.

**Key features.**
- Setup commands per agent: `engram setup claude-code`, `engram setup codex`, `engram setup cursor`, covering 14 setup targets in total, per the README.
- Data lives in `~/.engram/engram.db` (SQLite with FTS5) by default, overridable with an absolute `ENGRAM_DATA_DIR`.
- The README's "MCP tools" link describes 27 tools. The ones it documents include `mem_save`, `mem_search`, `mem_context`, `mem_timeline`, `mem_get_observation`, `mem_update`, `mem_session_summary` and `mem_doctor`.
- A "Memory Protocol" telling the agent to save decisions and fixes deliberately, give evolving topics a stable `topic_key`, and leave a session summary before ending, including after compaction.

**Pros**
- Simplest local install of the dedicated options: `brew install gentleman-programming/tap/engram`, then one setup command.
- MIT, no account, no telemetry mention in the parts I read, and a TUI for browsing memories.
- Designed around compaction recovery, a real pain point in long Claude Code sessions.

**Cons**
- Capture depends on the agent following the protocol. In Cursor, setup writes the protocol to a file and the docs say you must paste it into User Rules yourself; Cursor does not load it automatically.
- I found no hosted or sync plan, so sharing across machines is on you.
- 27 tools is a lot of surface; the README notes tool availability varies by MCP profile, and Cursor setup uses `--tools=agent` to narrow it.

**Benefits for your use case.** A local, inspectable memory that survives context compaction, with first-class Claude Code and Codex setup.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026).** Free and open source (MIT). I found no paid plan.

**Free tier / trial.** Everything is free.

**Standout use case.** Solo developers who want durable decisions and bug-fix notes in a local SQLite file.

**Skip it if** you want fully automatic capture without prompting the agent, or a cloud-synced memory.

**Setup (from the README and AGENT-SETUP.md).**

```bash
brew install gentleman-programming/tap/engram
engram setup claude-code   # or: engram setup codex / engram setup cursor
```

Codex setup registers `[mcp_servers.engram]` in `~/.codex/config.toml` and installs the Codex plugin. The manual Cursor config, from the docs:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "engram": {
      "command": "engram",
      "args": ["mcp", "--tools=agent"]
    }
  }
}
```

For Claude Code, the docs also offer a plugin route (`claude plugin marketplace add Gentleman-Programming/engram`, then `claude plugin install engram`), and note it installs plugin assets only and does not register the MCP server, so use `engram setup claude-code` or the manual MCP setup alongside it.

## 8. agentmemory: hooks, 54 MCP tools and keyless search

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/agentmemory.png" name="agentmemory" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** agentmemory (rohitg00/agentmemory, Apache-2.0) is a local memory server for coding agents with hooks, skills and an MCP bridge. It had 30,607 stars on November 26, 2026, and its latest release, v0.9.33, was published November 9, 2026. The repository description calls it the number one persistent memory for coding agents "based on real-world benchmarks", which is the author's own claim.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want automatic capture plus a large MCP tool set, running entirely on their machine, and who do not mind a local daemon.

**Key features.**
- For Claude Code the README describes a native plugin with 12 hooks, 17 skills and an auto-wired stdio MCP server exposing 54 MCP tools.
- Keyless mode: no model API key needed. In that mode vector embeddings are off and `memory_recall` uses BM25; setting `EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=local` in `~/.agentmemory/.env` adds free on-device semantic recall.
- A local API on `http://localhost:3111` and a live viewer on `http://localhost:3113`, both bound to loopback by default.
- Support listed for Claude Code, Copilot CLI, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, Hermes, OpenClaw, OpenCode and any MCP client.

**Pros**
- Free, Apache-2.0, nothing leaves your machine by default.
- Publishes a retrieval benchmark with its method and limits: on LongMemEval-S (measured October 28, 2026, 500 questions, 470 answerable), the keyless BM25 component found every required source for 84.7 percent of answerable questions versus 72.1 percent for a lexical baseline, and at least one relevant source for 96.2 percent versus 88.7 percent. This is vendor-reported, and the README itself says it tests retrieval only, not full-system answer quality or competitor rankings.
- Secret scrubbing and offline replay are listed for captured events.

**Cons**
- 54 tools is a big surface for an agent to choose from; tool-selection cost is real.
- It runs a local daemon (plus a viewer) that you have to keep alive, so there is more to operate than a stdio-only server.
- A documented gotcha: wiring the MCP server straight into `~/.claude.json` without `/plugin install` means Claude Code never resolves `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}`, so hook scripts need absolute paths.
- The README's own heads-up says `import-jsonl` is a weak primary capture path, because Claude Code's `cleanupPeriodDays` setting (default 30) deletes older transcripts.

**Benefits for your use case.** It gets Claude Code and Codex automatic capture without an API key, and gives you a viewer to inspect and edit what it stored.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026).** Free and open source. I found no hosted plan.

**Free tier / trial.** Everything is free; LLM-written observation compression is opt-in, so any model cost is yours and optional.

**Standout use case.** Offline, keyless memory for a developer who works across Claude Code, Codex and Cursor on the same projects.

**Skip it if** you want a minimal stdio server, or you do not want a background process and a second localhost port.

**Setup (from the README).**

```bash
npx -y @agentmemory/agentmemory@latest
/plugin marketplace add rohitg00/agentmemory
/plugin install agentmemory
```

```bash
codex mcp add agentmemory -- npx -y @agentmemory/mcp

codex plugin marketplace add rohitg00/agentmemory
codex plugin add agentmemory@agentmemory
```

For Cursor the README lists a full plugin through the Cursor Marketplace listing or Settings, Plugins, local checkout. For MCP-only clients the README says to use the same `mcpServers` block; the stdio command is `npx -y @agentmemory/mcp`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "agentmemory": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@agentmemory/mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

{% figure src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rohitg00/agentmemory/main/assets/banner.png" alt="agentmemory banner from the project repository" caption="agentmemory repository banner. Source: github.com/rohitg00/agentmemory" /%}

## 9. Letta: a stateful-agent platform, not a plug-in memory server

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/letta.png" name="Letta" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Letta (formerly MemGPT) is a platform for stateful agents with memory that learns over time. The `letta-ai/letta` repository (Apache-2.0) had 25,561 stars on November 26, 2026, but its README says the current source lives in `letta-ai/letta-code`, which holds the agent harness, terminal UI and App Server. The older Letta V1 API server is on an `archive` branch. So today Letta competes with Claude Code more than it plugs into it.

**Why it is in a list of memory MCP servers.** Because many people search for it alongside these tools. I include it for completeness and to be clear about the trade-off: you adopt Letta's agent, you do not bolt it onto yours. Letta's docs do have a section on configuring MCP servers inside Letta Code, but that is Letta consuming MCP, which is a different direction from the other nine products here. I did not find a Letta-published MCP memory server for Claude Code or Codex.

**Who it is for.** Teams building long-lived agents who want memory as a first-class, self-editing part of the runtime, with memory repositories (the docs have a section called MemFS).

**Key features.**
- Install with `npm install -g @letta-ai/letta-code`, run `letta` for the terminal UI or `letta server` for the App Server.
- Access through chat.letta.com, Slack, Telegram, Discord, custom channels and an Agent SDK for TypeScript.
- Letta Cloud keeps agent memory, identity and conversations available across computers.

**Pros**
- The most developed idea of agent-managed memory in this list.
- Free plan lets you bring your own API keys or an external coding plan such as ChatGPT/Codex, Kimi or Z.AI.
- Apache-2.0 source.

**Cons**
- It is a harness swap. If you want Claude Code's own tooling and models, Letta is not an add-on.
- The old server API is retired, so older tutorials and the `letta-ai/letta` README's history will mislead you. The last tagged release on that repository, 0.16.8, is dated May 14, 2026.
- Free-plan storage is small (5 MiB per memory repository).

**Benefits for your use case.** If you are choosing a runtime for persistent agents rather than adding memory to Claude Code, Letta shows what first-class memory looks like.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, docs.letta.com pricing page).** Free $0, up to 3 stateful agents, 5 MiB of storage per memory repository, bring your own keys. Pro $20 per month with $20 in monthly credits, remote sandboxes charged against credits, up to 20 stateful agents and 50 MiB per memory repository. Teams Pro $20 per seat per month (shared agents and permissions). Enterprise is custom, with SSO and role-based access. Cloud sandbox runtime is billed separately at 1,000 credits per dollar.

**Free tier / trial.** Free plan as above.

**Standout use case.** A persistent assistant that edits its own memory, run as the agent itself.

**Skip it if** you want to keep using Claude Code or Codex as your main agent and only add memory to them.

**Setup (from the README).**

```bash
npm install -g @letta-ai/letta-code
letta          # interactive terminal UI
letta server   # App Server for local or self-hosted agents
```

## 10. MCP memory server (official reference): the small baseline

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-memory-mcp-servers-for-claude-code-2026/logos/mcp-memory-server.png" name="MCP memory server" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A knowledge-graph memory server maintained in the `modelcontextprotocol/servers` repository, published on npm as `@modelcontextprotocol/server-memory`. It stores entities, relations and observations in a JSONL file. The repository had 92,277 stars on November 26, 2026, though that counts the whole repository of reference servers, not the memory server alone.

**Who it is for.** Anyone who wants the simplest possible persistent memory, or wants to read a clean reference implementation before choosing a heavier tool.

**Key features.**
- Nine tools: `create_entities`, `create_relations`, `add_observations`, `delete_entities`, `delete_observations`, `delete_relations`, `read_graph`, `search_nodes` and `open_nodes`.
- Storage in a JSONL file whose location you control with `MEMORY_FILE_PATH`.
- A file lock next to the memory file so several clients writing to one file do not corrupt it.
- Runs through `npx`, Docker, or a Streamable HTTP mode.

**Pros**
- Tiny, free and boring, with no account and no database.
- The same server works in Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and anything else that speaks MCP.
- Documented environment variable and upgrade guidance for Docker.

**Cons**
- The repository README says its servers are reference implementations and educational examples, not production-ready solutions, and tells developers to evaluate their own security requirements.
- The agent must decide what to store and when; there is no automatic capture and no semantic search, only name and observation matching through `search_nodes`.
- The default file location sits next to the server's code, so under `npx` it can end up in a cache directory. The README says to set `MEMORY_FILE_PATH` to an absolute path you own.
- Requires Node.js 24 or later when run through `npx` or from source, per the README.

**Benefits for your use case.** A cheap way to test whether persistent graph memory helps your workflow before you commit to a bigger system.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026).** Free. Licensing is mixed: the repo's license notice says the MCP project is moving from MIT to Apache-2.0, with new code under Apache-2.0 and older contributions that have not been relicensed remaining under MIT. GitHub's license detector reports "NOASSERTION" for the repository for that reason.

**Free tier / trial.** Everything is free.

**Standout use case.** A starter memory for one person, or a teaching example for your own server.

**Skip it if** you want automatic capture, ranking or anything resembling semantic recall.

**Setup (from the README, plus standard client syntax).**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "memory": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-memory"],
      "env": {
        "MEMORY_FILE_PATH": "/path/to/custom/memory.jsonl"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

That JSON is the README's config and works in Cursor's `.cursor/mcp.json`. For Claude Code and Codex, the equivalents are my translation of that config:

```bash
claude mcp add memory --env MEMORY_FILE_PATH=$HOME/memory.jsonl -- npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-memory
```

```toml
[mcp_servers.memory]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-memory"]
env = { MEMORY_FILE_PATH = "/home/you/memory.jsonl" }
```

