## Feature matrix

"Yes", "No" and "Partial" reflect what each project's own README or docs say as of November 26, 2026. I did not test every cell myself.

| Feature | claude-mem | Basic Memory | Mem0 | Supermemory | Graphiti / Zep | Cognee | Engram | agentmemory | MCP memory | Letta |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Automatic capture | Yes | Partial (opt-in) | Yes (plugin) | Yes (plugins) | No | Yes (plugins) | Partial (protocol) | Yes | No | Partial (agent-managed) |
| Runs fully local | Yes | Yes | No (hosted) | Partial (local mode) | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Partial |
| Open source core | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Graphiti yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Claude Code setup documented | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | Yes | Yes | Generic | No |
| Codex setup documented | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Yes | Yes | Yes | Generic | No |
| Cursor setup documented | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Generic | No |
| Graph memory | No | Partial | Custom plan | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Partial | Yes | Not checked |
| Semantic or vector search | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | No | Optional | No | Not checked |
| Hosted option | Yes (sync) | Yes | Yes | Yes | Zep | Yes | No | No | No | Yes |
| Human-readable store | No | Yes | No | No | No | No | No (SQLite, TUI) | Partial (viewer) | Yes (JSONL) | Not checked |

Notes on the less obvious cells. "Partial" for Basic Memory capture reflects the README's "opt-in capture" in its Claude Code plugin; the default is that the model writes notes. Engram's "No" for semantic search reflects SQLite FTS5 full-text search in the README, with no vector mention in the sections I read. Graphiti's Claude Code and Codex cells are "No" because I found documented snippets only for Claude Desktop, Cursor and VS Code, though any MCP-capable client can use its HTTP endpoint.

## Head-to-head: which to pick

### claude-mem vs agentmemory: which to pick

Both capture sessions through hooks and run locally under Apache-2.0. claude-mem has far more stars (119,949 against 30,607 on November 26, 2026) and a paid sync option; agentmemory exposes many more MCP tools (54) and publishes a retrieval benchmark with its limits stated. Pick claude-mem if you want the larger community, an installer that covers Claude Code, Codex and Cursor in one command, and the option to sync across machines. Pick agentmemory if you want keyless operation with a local viewer and are comfortable running a second localhost service. Both are heavier than a stdio-only server.

### Basic Memory vs the official MCP memory server: which to pick

Both are MCP servers that the model writes to explicitly. Basic Memory stores Markdown files you can open in any editor, offers semantic search and a Claude Code plugin, and has a paid cloud. The official server stores a JSONL graph file, has nine simple tools and no ranking. Pick Basic Memory for a knowledge base you will read and curate. Pick the official server to test the idea in minutes, or to read the code.

### Mem0 vs Supermemory: which to pick

Both are hosted memory APIs with an MCP endpoint, plugins for Claude Code and Codex, and a free plan. Mem0's open-source core has more stars (69,508 against 31,837), and its hosted free plan is described in monthly add and retrieval limits. Supermemory prices in usage credits, includes all plugins on the free plan, and offers a local mode and a self-hosted option on Scale. Pick Mem0 if you want the familiar add and search model with a hosted MCP endpoint. Pick Supermemory if you want a shared memory across more coding agents and a way to run it locally.

### Graphiti / Zep vs Cognee: which to pick

Both are graph memory, both Apache-2.0 at the core. Graphiti's strength is bi-temporal facts and a precise tool set; its MCP server is a reference implementation needing a graph database. Cognee's strength is Claude Code and Codex plugins and a small `remember`, `recall`, `forget` tool surface. Pick Graphiti when facts expire and provenance matters. Pick Cognee when you want graph memory with plugin-level setup for Claude Code and Codex.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

These are real entries from the agentskills.codes catalog (each page returned HTTP 200 on November 26, 2026). Install counts in the catalog were low (single or low double digits for most of these), so treat them as options to evaluate, not endorsements.

- [mem-search](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mem-search): published by thedotmack, the claude-mem author. Searches claude-mem's cross-session memory when you ask "did we already solve this?". The natural companion to product 1.
- [super-search](https://agentskills.codes/skills/super-search): published by supermemoryai. Searches your coding memory for past work and earlier sessions. Pairs with Supermemory's plugins.
- [memory-mcp](https://agentskills.codes/skills/memory-mcp): by d-o-hub. Explains how to use and troubleshoot a Memory MCP server for episodic retrieval. Useful with the official reference server.
- [agent-memory-mcp](https://agentskills.codes/skills/agent-memory-mcp): by davila7. A hybrid memory system for persistent, searchable knowledge such as architecture, patterns and decisions.
- [memory-systems](https://agentskills.codes/skills/memory-systems): by sickn33. Covers short-term, long-term and graph-based memory architectures; good background before choosing between notes, vectors and a graph.
- [conversation-memory](https://agentskills.codes/skills/conversation-memory): by davila7. Short-term, long-term and entity-based memory patterns for LLM conversations.
- [enhance-claude-memory](https://agentskills.codes/skills/enhance-claude-memory): by avifenesh. For improving CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md project memory files, which you should tidy before adding any MCP server.
- [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder): by anthropics. A guide to building high-quality MCP servers, useful if you outgrow the reference server and want your own.

