## Best for X: decision table

| You are | Pick | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A solo developer who wants zero effort | claude-mem | Hooks capture automatically; local by default |
| Someone who wants to read and edit memory | Basic Memory | Plain Markdown files |
| Someone who wants a free, local, minimal install | Engram | One binary, SQLite, MIT |
| A team that wants a managed API | Mem0 or Supermemory | Hosted MCP endpoints and plugins |
| Using Claude Code, Codex and Cursor together | Supermemory or claude-mem | Both document all three |
| Someone whose facts change over time | Graphiti / Zep | Validity windows on facts |
| Someone who wants graph memory with plugins | Cognee | Claude Code and Codex plugins |
| Someone who needs no API key and a viewer | agentmemory | Keyless BM25 and a local viewer |
| Someone just trying the idea | MCP memory server | Smallest possible setup |
| Building a long-lived agent runtime | Letta | Memory is part of the agent itself |

## Common mistakes

- **Installing two automatic-capture systems at once.** claude-mem, agentmemory, the Mem0 plugin and the Supermemory plugin each register hooks. Running several means duplicate capture and competing context. The Mem0 docs even warn against combining its Codex plugin with the manual MCP block.
- **Treating `npm install -g claude-mem` as the install.** The README says it installs the SDK only; use `npx claude-mem install` or the plugin marketplace.
- **Leaving the official memory server's file path at the default.** Set `MEMORY_FILE_PATH` to an absolute path you own, or your memory may live in an npx cache.
- **Searching for "OpenMemory" and installing the old project.** The local OpenMemory in the Mem0 repository was removed, as described in section 3.
- **Trusting vendor benchmark claims side by side.** LongMemEval, LoCoMo, ConvoMem and DolphinBench are run by the vendors that cite them, with different setups.
- **Putting secrets into memory.** Anything an agent captures can be stored. Check what each tool scrubs and where it stores data before pointing it at a private repository.
- **Ignoring built-in memory first.** Claude Code's own CLAUDE.md and auto memory cover a lot. Keep CLAUDE.md short (Anthropic's docs suggest under 200 lines) before adding a server.

## Buying and setup checklist

1. Decide whether memory must stay on your machine. If yes, shortlist claude-mem, Basic Memory, Engram, agentmemory, Graphiti, or the official server.
2. Decide who writes memory: automatic hooks (claude-mem, agentmemory, Mem0 and Supermemory plugins) or the agent on request (Basic Memory, Engram, the official server).
3. Check licenses. Basic Memory is AGPL-3.0; most others are Apache-2.0 or MIT.
4. Start with one tool in one client. Confirm it saves something and recalls it in a fresh session before adding a second client.
5. Read what leaves your machine: hosted MCP endpoints send content to the vendor, and some tools send anonymous telemetry unless disabled.
6. Put the config in the right file: `.mcp.json` or `claude mcp add` for Claude Code, `~/.codex/config.toml` for Codex, `.cursor/mcp.json` for Cursor.
7. Write down how to remove it. Mem0's docs show an uninstall command for its plugin; check the others before you rely on one.
8. Re-check pricing before you subscribe. Basic Memory's README says $15.00 per month locked for life while its pricing page lists $19, so vendor pages can disagree with each other.

## How we evaluated

This is a documentation-and-sources review, not a lab test. On November 26, 2026 I did the following:

- Pulled repository metadata (stars, licenses, latest release, last push) from the GitHub API for all ten projects.
- Read each project's README and, where relevant, its install, MCP and setup documents, and copied configuration from them. Where a project did not publish a snippet for Claude Code or Codex, I translated the same endpoint into that client's standard syntax and labelled it as my translation.
- Fetched each vendor's pricing page and recorded plans and limits. 
- Checked each YouTube video through YouTube's oEmbed endpoint and each X post through the syndication endpoint, and fetched each image URL to confirm an image content type.
- Checked every internal agentskills.codes page for an HTTP 200.

I did not install and run these tools against a real Claude Code or Codex session for this post, so I make no claims about speed, recall quality or reliability beyond what the vendors state, and I label those as vendor-reported. The ranking is a judgment based on install friction, documented client support, openness and clarity about limits. There are no affiliate links, and no vendor paid for placement.

## FAQ

### What is the best memory MCP server for Claude Code?

For most developers, claude-mem is the easiest and most popular choice: it captures sessions automatically, runs locally and is Apache-2.0. If you want to read and edit memory as Markdown, pick Basic Memory. If you want a hosted API, Mem0 and Supermemory both document Claude Code setup.

### What is the best memory MCP for Codex?

Several projects document Codex directly: claude-mem, Basic Memory (a `[mcp_servers.basic-memory]` TOML block), Mem0 (`codex mcp add mem0 --url https://mcp.mem0.ai/mcp/ --bearer-token-env-var MEM0_API_KEY`), Cognee, Engram (`engram setup codex`), agentmemory and Supermemory. Codex reads MCP servers from `~/.codex/config.toml`, so any stdio or HTTP server can be added there.

### Do I still need a memory server if Claude Code has auto memory?

Maybe not. Claude Code's auto memory is machine-local, loads only the first 200 lines or 25KB of MEMORY.md at session start, and is not shared across machines or cloud environments, according to Anthropic's docs. A memory server helps when you need cross-machine continuity, searchable history, other agents sharing the same memory, or structured knowledge.

### What are the main types of AI agent memory?

A common split is short-term (the current context window), long-term (stored facts that persist), episodic (what happened in past sessions), semantic (facts and concepts) and procedural (how to do things, often as skills or rules). In this list, claude-mem and Engram lean episodic, Basic Memory and the official server hold semantic notes, Graphiti and Cognee model facts and relations, and CLAUDE.md files and agent skills cover procedural memory. The [memory-systems](https://agentskills.codes/skills/memory-systems) skill walks through these architectures.

### Is OpenMemory MCP still available?

Not as a local project inside Mem0. A merged Mem0 pull request removed the `openmemory/` directory on July 29, 2026 and points self-hosters to the `server/` directory. The `mem0ai/openmemory` repository now describes a different tool, a CLI and TUI for moving coding sessions between Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode. For Mem0 memory today, use the hosted MCP endpoint or the plugins.

### Which of these are fully free and open source?

Engram (MIT), agentmemory (Apache-2.0), the official MCP memory server (Apache-2.0 for new code, MIT for older), Graphiti (Apache-2.0), claude-mem (Apache-2.0, engine free), Basic Memory (AGPL-3.0, free locally) and Cognee (Apache-2.0). Mem0 and Supermemory have open-source cores but their hosted services are the easy path, and Letta has an Apache-2.0 repository but a hosted pricing model.

### Is my data sent to the cloud?

Hosted endpoints (Mem0, Supermemory, Zep, Cognee Cloud) send memory content to the vendor. Local options store data on your machine, but graph-building tools such as Graphiti and Cognee call whatever LLM you configure, so content goes to that model provider. claude-mem's README says anonymous usage analytics are on by default and can be disabled with `npx claude-mem telemetry disable`.

### How much does memory cost per month?

The self-hosted options cost nothing beyond model usage you choose. Hosted entry points as of November 26, 2026: Basic Memory Cloud $19, Supermemory Pro $19, Letta Pro $20, claude-mem Cloud Sync $12, Zep Flex $125. Mem0 and Cognee have free plans with limits, and Cognee Standard includes 100M tokens.

### Can I use more than one memory server?

Yes, but avoid running more than one automatic-capture system at once. A common setup is one automatic tool (claude-mem or a plugin) for history, plus a notes tool (Basic Memory) or the official server for deliberate knowledge. Keep each in its own namespace and check that tool names do not collide.

### What is the difference between a memory MCP server and a skill?

An MCP server is a running process that exposes tools, such as search and save. A skill is a folder of instructions the agent loads on demand. They work together: [mem-search](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mem-search) is a skill that tells the agent how to use claude-mem's search tools. The catalog on agentskills.codes lists skills like these.

### How do I stop an agent from remembering something?

Each tool has a delete path: the official server has `delete_entities`, `delete_observations` and `delete_relations`; Mem0's MCP exposes `delete_memory` and `delete_all_memories`; Cognee has `forget`; agentmemory has a viewer to edit, export and forget memories. For file-based tools such as Basic Memory, you delete the note.

